WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.140
Hi, my name is Rhea Karam. I'm a visual
artist, bookmaker, and photographer.

00:00:04.519 --> 00:00:06.160
And today, we're in my studio,

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.260
and we're gonna be demonstrating
how to make an artist book,

00:00:09.619 --> 00:00:12.940
from designing the book,
making a book dummy, printing,

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.139
and putting the book together.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.260
I used to be a staff photographer at
Christie's. That was my first job,

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.100
and I photographed artworks. And one
day, I was given a stack of books,

00:00:26.799 --> 00:00:30.920
and amongst those books, there was
a book by Sonia Delaunay from 1913,

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.940
and it's a small scale book that just
expands. It extends over six feet.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.440
That's also something that is
very particular to artist books,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.880
is that it can vary in scale, it's not
fixed. So that book, which is very small,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.159
and you wouldn't really
know what's inside,

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.720
can pull out and become
something much larger.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.659
You don't know what you're gonna come
across or discover when you open the book,

00:00:53.819 --> 00:00:55.360
and that's part of the
pleasure of making it.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.999
So we're gonna start with
the design process. Usually,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.700
I have a lot of ideas in my mind, and
a lot of materials I wanna work with,

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:08.560
and then it's about figuring out how
to sequence it and what can work,

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.459
what can't. I'm just gonna plot out a
little sketch of what I'm thinking to do,

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.920
which is a book on each side.

00:01:16.319 --> 00:01:19.840
So we'll have a left side and a right
side that open up together and make it

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.000
feel a bit more like a ballad.
That's the idea for today.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.900
I always kind of sketch
it out laying down,

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.060
and then what it would
look like when it's open,

00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:32.280
and how it would look standing as well.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.420
But in the sketching
things are still really,

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.400
really loose and fun in the creative
part before we reach the part that's a

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.360
little more pragmatic. For the
book we're gonna make today,

00:01:41.459 --> 00:01:44.959
we're gonna use photographs that I've
taken of walls throughout the city of New

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.240
York. My work has been primarily around
public walls, photographing them,

00:01:49.739 --> 00:01:50.680
wheatpasting them.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.860
So I will be collecting
certain photographs and
looking at them and bringing

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:55.713
them together.

00:01:58.199 --> 00:02:00.980
Once the design and the
drawing is kind of ready,

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.679
I move onto thinking about
how to make a book dummy.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.520
And a book dummy is really about
making a miniature mock-up.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:11.820
I produce a little book and see how it
works, play with it in all its forms.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.320
And that also gives me ideas for the
larger book that maybe in the sketch I

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.139
wouldn't have thought of, but when I
make the dummy, it kind of comes to life.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.180
And so usually I'll make
about three to four dummies.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.300
It really depends on the book.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.760
I had this idea of casting a
book in a concrete cement brick.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.020
The brick acts as a
slip cover for the book.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.919
And this was a lot of trial and
error. I did resin, I did 3D printing,

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.220
I also did book board where
I had the book inside.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:42.639
It's a lot of different material
testing until you reach the final

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:44.780
destination. And that's part of the fun.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:50.080
This is the dummy that I kind of
prepped so you can kind of see how it

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.340
unfolds. The idea is you
could flip through the right
or flip through the left,

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:58.100
and there's multiple ways to view it.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.460
Now I'm going to go into my folder with
all the images that I've selected for

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:06.280
the book.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.900
There is a very mathematical aspect to
bookmaking where measurements really

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.080
matter. I try to put as many spreads
on a page to not waste paper,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.320
as many that can fit for the format.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.520
I'm thinking of making a book that's
going to be around nine by nine inches or

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.560
eight by nine, so
something relatively small.

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:29.779
So here I've set it up on the page and
I've managed to put two on the page and I

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.258
have the crop marks indicated
so that once it's printed,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.200
I know exactly where to trim. An
important step as well is, uh,

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:41.300
the choice of paper. The best way
to look at paper is these swatches.

00:03:41.579 --> 00:03:43.200
You can get them at any photo store.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.419
It's really about the feel of the
paper because it's very tactile and the

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.520
weight of it. Should it be more
rigid? Should it have texture?

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:52.393
Should it be smooth?

00:03:52.619 --> 00:03:56.540
I think we're going to go with this photo
rag paper that has a really nice feel.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.320
Since they're artist books and they look
painterly, people come and touch them.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.159
So I like for the texture
to really be involved.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.520
So I'm going to send
that off to the printer.

00:04:11.559 --> 00:04:13.619
Now that the images are printed,

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.860
I'm going to think a little
bit about sequencing.

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:19.240
I always have little thumbnails
to look at everything,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:22.700
and then I use this
board as a kind of map.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.320
I choose the spreads that I
intend to have side by side.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:33.420
And sometimes changes are made if
they look better in a different order.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.000
Order is usually intuitive.

00:04:36.279 --> 00:04:40.140
I kind of look at color and form and
what part of the image is going to be on

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.339
the edge. So I always think about
the action of opening the book.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.760
And when you open it, what
is going to be on the edge,

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.480
what's going to be meeting the
other photograph on the other side?

00:04:48.779 --> 00:04:49.520
For this book,

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.060
I mentioned we're going to do a left and
a right side and they're going to kind

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.500
of act like a ballad between each other.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.480
So it's really important to know that
these two, once they're trimmed and ready,

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.820
are going to be overlapping
and side by side.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.360
And do they work in conversation or
not? And if they don't, you know,

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:06.880
sometimes I'll switch it
around and say, "Okay,

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:11.480
that could be better." And it's really
about just trying different things.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:12.980
So I think I like it better here

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.060
and I think I prefer it here.

00:05:17.339 --> 00:05:21.260
So it's a process which is
also pretty fun and playful.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.720
And then I just step back and see
it as a composition in a whole.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:30.700
In this book, it's really about
layering and corners of the city.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.180
So I'm keeping in mind that this
is one spread, a spread is a side,

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:36.620
and it's going to be folded in two.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:42.020
So I've chosen corners in areas that
are inherently folding in real life.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.013
It's a bit abstract,

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:47.700
but visually the idea that this is going
to be folded and a bit sculpturally

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.120
elevated from the flatness of the book.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.960
So now that we've
finished all the printing,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.440
we're gonna work on embellishing
the prints and getting them ready.

00:05:59.779 --> 00:06:03.700
I usually incorporate a lot of materials
that are inspired from the photographs

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:04.820
and the street materials.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:09.300
I brought out an example just to kind of
show the industrial materials I like to

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.480
play around and work with.
This is a tarp cover.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.740
It's an industrial belt sandpaper book.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.779
These are prints that
have been embellished with
spray paint where there is

00:06:17.839 --> 00:06:18.800
cutting involved.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:23.400
What I like about the spray paint
is there is irregularity. I mean,

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.920
at the end of the day, these are handmade
books, so it's really nice, I think,

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:30.400
to see that human touch. And it
makes the work very painterly.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:35.140
So you start to not know
what is photographed, what
is painted, what is added,

00:06:35.279 --> 00:06:36.880
and it leans into abstraction.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.640
One of the biggest references was
this photo shoot I had where I also

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:46.199
photographed Miró books. It was Miró's
sketches and paintings bound together.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.920
And it is a whole different experience
on how you view the work and having it

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.580
open and available at all
times versus on a wall.

00:06:53.839 --> 00:06:55.960
Another big reference is Tauba Auerbach,

00:06:56.279 --> 00:07:01.120
whose books are extremely sculptural
and sometimes it's just one gesture of

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.200
opening it and they take
shape and take form.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.599
So all these things are in my mind.

00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:09.279
This is the part where everything is
flat and we don't have the dimension yet.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.040
And so it's really about playing around
with the paper on a one-dimensional

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:19.220
scale, but thinking about how it will
react once it's put into a more sculptural

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:24.159
object. I'm thinking to add some spray
paint here, some industrial tapes,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.440
and play around a little bit.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.000
So when I have some spray painting
to do, I usually come outside,

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:36.660
shake up the paint, and we're
gonna spray paint this edge.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.660
So I kind of just prep a little
stencil that blocks out and

00:07:42.719 --> 00:07:47.460
protects the print, and then I just
line it up. And then we're just going to

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.899
get a nice solid color, pull that off.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:58.280
There the edge is painted and ready to go.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.820
Another artist book that I think is
really great is [by] John Baldessari,

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.780
who uses a lot of tape and paper
and pencil and it's almost like a

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:14.760
sketchbook. So although the books are
really thought out and precise, in a way,

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:19.540
there is play that is involved. So
now that this spray paint is dry,

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:23.879
we are going to apply this
tape to one of these prints.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.800
And what I like about tape
as well is it's very linear.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:35.580
You can kind of play with
lines and directions and
it's almost like drawing with

00:08:35.879 --> 00:08:36.712
color.

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:42.740
And then I'll just apply it parallel
to the lines that are on the

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:46.740
photograph. I extend
it over the crop marks,

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:51.360
so that'll be chopped off and it'll be
flush to the edge. And for this one,

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:54.720
I kind of wanted to play
with the metallic texture.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.679
So we're gonna try this foil tape.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:03.300
So what's really fun about bookmaking
is it's an open canvas to any

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:08.220
form of experimentation or material
you wanna bring to it. In my case,

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:12.880
it started out with photography and
then I started to incorporate other

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:17.260
materials that I was coming across in my
practice and it's kind of a place that

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:19.700
holds it all together with
the story you wanna tell.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.280
This is a tape that's
intended for electric,

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:29.240
so it's not really intended
for paper. It's very finicky.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.700
On this print, we're going
to add some collage work.

00:09:36.679 --> 00:09:39.760
I usually pull out scraps and kind
of play around with composition,

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.960
which is really what I do on the
street when I walk around pasting.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:48.040
A lot of times I paste on
corners, geometric forms.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:53.000
I like it to come back into the books.
So I think I'm gonna use the blue.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:58.620
I use this double-sided tape. Sometimes
I'll use glue. It really depends.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:05.179
And then lay this right on the edge
and then we get rid of the blue,

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:06.480
kind of scrubbing it.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.440
So here we have three prints that we
embellished with tape, spray paint,

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:14.280
and some collage work.
I'm gonna put them aside.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:18.720
Something I didn't speak about yet
is additioning for an artist book.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:22.360
I usually like making small editions,
but definitely more than one or two.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:25.620
There's something very
meditative about bookmaking.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:29.179
There's something I really like
about the repetition of also, um,

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:32.160
certain gestures. So if
we're taping and we have 30,

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.820
I would do them all one after the
other. Here we have the back page,

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:40.420
and I'm trying to figure out where
to put the title and the edition.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:42.059
I usually like to put it at the end.

00:10:42.179 --> 00:10:44.940
I like it for it to be a little
bit hidden or integrated.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:50.360
And I like this wall because it
also lends itself to tagging it.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.860
So I think we're gonna put
the edition number down here,

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:00.340
and then for the signature, we're gonna
put it up here. And so here we have it.

00:11:00.679 --> 00:11:04.140
It's kind of integrated into the
image, and I'll set this aside.

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:11.380
Now we're gonna turn a corner and we're
going to move on to the really technical

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:14.240
part of bookmaking. We're just
gonna start by trimming the prints.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.940
I use a roller trimmer.
I also have a guillotine.

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:20.660
You can just use a cutter
on a cutting board.

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:24.140
I didn't have all of this
equipment when I first started.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.800
I was cutting everything with an
Xacto knife. I didn't have a press,

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.340
so I just used bricks to push down
paper. And it's just with time, you know,

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:35.860
if you start making larger quantities
that you need to invest in certain

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:40.160
machines that make your life easier, but
otherwise it's really quite accessible,

00:11:40.179 --> 00:11:41.920
which is something I really like.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:47.880
When you do embellish the prints,
it's never easy to get a perfect edge,

00:11:48.559 --> 00:11:51.140
so you can go over the borders with
the paint, with whatever it is,

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:52.860
and then you bring it
back in with the trimming.

00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:58.640
I do have crop marks on each side, so
sometimes I'll crop just to the edge,

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.760
but not fully so it doesn't crop the
crop mark in the other direction off.

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:03.800
But once I have two edges,

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:08.040
then I have the measurements of the
corner and I can do it without both crop

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:12.980
marks. And I know that my book is nine
inches high, so I just go to the nine,

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:14.020
make sure it's lined up.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:21.740
It's really nice to have this balance
of two kind of processes that in

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:25.800
a way are very opposite to each
other. After all the creative process,

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:29.740
you enter the more structured part of
the bookmaking process and everything's

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:33.140
pretty technical. When you work
as a freelancer, as an artist,

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:36.280
there's a lot of times where you lack
structure and you're kind of on your own

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:37.153
doing your own thing.

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:41.640
And I really like how the book
gives you some kind of structure,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:43.679
like you come in knowing
exactly what you're gonna do.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.460
So now that our prints
are trimmed and ready,

00:12:50.059 --> 00:12:51.220
I'm gonna put them in the right order.

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:54.620
So I did a little visual so I can remember
the order and I'm gonna number them

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:58.500
on the back. So we're working on
the left side. So this is L1, L2,

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:04.120
L3. Okay.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:09.700
So now the prints are numbered. We're
gonna start scoring and folding.

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:13.820
It's done here on this, what we call a
scoring board, and I have a bone folder,

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:18.900
which is a tool we're gonna use to
basically create a crease in the paper.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:23.240
You take your print and you
place it right along the edges.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.780
We know that we're
eight and a half across,

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.780
and we wanna score it right in the
middle, so that's four and a quarter.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:36.620
So I have to look at the four
and a quarter, and as you see,

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:38.700
that kinda lines up with
the edge of the photograph,

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:42.800
you put the pointy side of the
bone folder right in that line,

00:13:43.979 --> 00:13:46.920
and you just drag it
down slowly and steady.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.940
And I like to go twice.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:56.559
Now the paper closes and you take
the bone folder and you push down

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.900
with a bit of pressure
so that it lays flat.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:06.080
The scoring board is really helpful in
terms of measurements and being precise.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:09.780
There's a little gap at every line,
so it basically helps you create a,

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:12.980
a very straight line when
you're scoring the paper.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:20.500
And what I'm doing here is a
binding method that's called perfect

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:21.740
binding,

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:26.319
and that's basically when the
paper is bound back to back

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:29.160
with double-sided tape or adhesive,

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:34.580
which is a binding method I really like
to use because it allows the spreads to

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:39.660
sit flat and there's no
kind of interference with a
thread or a staple and it's

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:41.020
really a clean binding.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:46.220
Here we have our book kind of
coming together and it's all

00:14:46.319 --> 00:14:50.100
scored and folded, so we're ready
to put the glue in adhesive.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:54.000
What we're gonna be doing is placing
double-sided tape along the edges.

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:57.540
We're gonna start with the spine
side and then move to the front.

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:00.400
So I kind of scatter them evenly.

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:05.959
The idea with tape, what's really
cool is it's already a straight line.

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:09.640
I just drag it down and then when
I reach the edge, I just cut it.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.760
These are archival tapes
that are double-sided,

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.500
but very easy to use.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:23.500
And so now we're gonna do the
same, but on the other side.

00:15:23.759 --> 00:15:26.500
All of these parts are
what really take time.

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:28.440
When you're doing a larger addition,

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.499
it's always nice to set
up some kind of a system.

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:34.620
I usually work on several
tables. So one is for trimming,

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:39.360
one is for putting the adhesive, and
sometimes, you know, you get bored of one,

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:40.433
at least I do, so I,

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:44.480
I skip from one table to another
and it makes it more interesting.

00:15:47.939 --> 00:15:52.740
You wanna try to line up as close to
the edge as you can because that's

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.540
gonna be the edge of your pages and
you don't want the gap to open up.

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:07.420
So now the important thing is to shake and

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:11.740
stack it up. You gotta kind
of make sure this is straight,

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:13.720
because this is gonna be
your spine and your edge.

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:16.640
I clamp it together just
to hold the book in place.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:20.740
This is the edge that we're not
gonna trim, which is the spine.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:24.140
So that's really the side you're gonna
focus on to make sure it's straight.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:29.880
We're gonna slowly lift the
edges and just pull off the tape.

00:16:30.119 --> 00:16:34.159
Once I pull this off, I put
this down and I just press.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:37.440
And that sticks those two pages together,

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:39.660
and then you go through the whole stack.

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:44.000
And the last page,

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:50.060
now our spine is stuck together.
We can remove the clamps.

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:52.060
You can press it down.

00:16:53.459 --> 00:16:56.159
And now the next step is to do this.

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:58.679
And this is much easier
because it's already glued,

00:16:58.820 --> 00:17:00.220
so it's not gonna shift in any way.

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:04.199
You just wanna make sure you press down
evenly and you're not twisting the paper

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:05.093
while you're doing that.

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:20.900
So here we have our book that's bound.

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:24.780
What I really like about Perfect
Binding, if you look at the spine,

00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:29.300
it's a very clean and minimal look,
so you don't see any stitching,

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:30.820
you don't have any glue here,

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:35.240
which works well with my photography
and the aesthetic that I'm trying to

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.220
achieve in the book. The
other thing with this binding,

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:41.800
which is one of the main reasons I'm
attracted to it, is it lays flat.

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.320
So now we have our left side ready,

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:53.320
and I prepared the right side in
advance so we can see how it kind

00:17:53.359 --> 00:17:55.720
of plays and it's
starting to come to life.

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:06.580
Now that we have the inside of the book
ready, we can start with the covers.

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:11.520
I though it could be kind of fun to
use the cardboards that I use for spray

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:12.780
painting outside. You know,

00:18:12.859 --> 00:18:17.240
they end up being really beautiful
and also spontaneous little

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:18.580
compositions.

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:23.500
So what I'm gonna do is make two
long rectangular covers for the front

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:25.280
and then one for the back.

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:29.940
So I'm gonna go over here and
kind of mark off the edges.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:36.340
The cover of the book is somewhere
where I find it really fun

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:41.840
to try something unusual and new
because it's really your first

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:46.520
exposure to the book. So
now we have the outline,

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:50.780
make sure the measurement's right.
It's eight and a half. And then again,

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:54.660
we're gonna cut it at four
and a quarter for the middle,

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:57.240
and then four and a quarter.

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:01.340
And there we have the center of the
book where we're gonna be cutting it.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.340
You can just take a cutter and line it up

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:10.873
And cut out your shape.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:16.619
A lot of the work that I'm doing in this
project has to do with the street and

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:20.780
the random encounters and of certain
corners and the paint and the colors,

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:23.859
even though it seems like I'm
just using recycled cardboard,

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:28.160
it is quite intentional and really is
in conversation with the work that's

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:32.700
inside. Similar to, um,
Giorgio de Chirico book,

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:37.940
"Defense de Savoir," "Forbidden
Knowledge," he uses very experimental

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:41.820
materials as well on the cover,
broken glass between sheets of lead,

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:47.420
which is something that adds both to
the title and conceptually to the book.

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:52.280
There's that element of danger that's
translated through the use of material.

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:55.100
So that's gonna be for
our two front covers.

00:19:55.359 --> 00:19:58.400
This is the back page that we
printed. So if you remember,

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:02.880
it was a double spread. Double
spread means that when it's folded,

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:05.080
both pages should fit in.

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:10.220
So we're gonna score this one and what
we're gonna do is glue this to the back

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:14.780
cover to give it some weight and then
we'll combine the other covers on top.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:19.820
So we bring back the scoring board
four and a quarter. It lines right up,

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:23.800
so it's the same measurement.
And we bring that

00:20:25.420 --> 00:20:29.359
and then we flip it and we
also at four and a quarter

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:36.740
Score it. So now we have
the back cover ready.

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:40.340
We're gonna fold the back pages. You know,

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:44.700
it's once you start producing the book
and going through this that you realize

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:46.960
that the measurements are really key.

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:49.560
If you're off by the
slightest measurement,

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:55.040
it can throw off the functionality of
the book. Now we have our three parts.

00:20:55.859 --> 00:20:59.820
So this part is going to glue onto this
part and it's gonna bring that into the

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:01.820
book and similar to the other side.

00:21:01.900 --> 00:21:06.020
What we need to do is place
the double-sided tape.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.820
All right. So now everything
is glued and ready.

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:16.180
Instead of cutting something out,

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:20.320
I'm gonna use this board so we
mix and match the materials.

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:23.740
I'm gonna put extra tape on the back.

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:28.320
I studied photography, but I've
always been interested in books.

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:33.040
When I first had my idea for my first
artist book, I worked with Small Editions,

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:35.020
which was a publishing house in Brooklyn.

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:39.140
They work with book binders and they
bound my first book. With time, you know,

00:21:39.220 --> 00:21:43.560
I wanted to learn how to do it myself
and it was really by just watching videos

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:45.460
and being self-taught on YouTube video.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:49.660
Earlier we put the signature and the
addition number inside and the date,

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:54.600
but I think it's nice to have
something on the outside with the name.

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.140
I have a stencil and I'm
thinking of putting it in.

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:58.460
So we're gonna do that first.

00:21:59.619 --> 00:22:02.400
So now I'm just playing a
little bit with placement.

00:22:03.119 --> 00:22:08.020
Why don't we try the placement up
here? We'll just put some scotch,

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:09.560
stick it into place,

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:15.320
and use this white acrylic paint.
So I don't want it to be too thick.

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:19.359
Gonna dab it first and let's
see how this turns out.

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:29.359
Okay. Seems to have turned out
okay. Yeah, that's perfect for this.

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:34.320
We're gonna start by letting this
dry and we're gonna bring the back

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:38.400
page of the book and just
stick that, kind of line it up.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:43.520
And I'm gonna start by removing one
side of the tape so that it sticks into

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:45.940
place and then it'll be
easier to glue all the rest.

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:47.520
I'm just gonna press it down.

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:55.400
So now we have one part down and I'm
just gonna remove all the other tape.

00:22:56.900 --> 00:22:57.900
Usually I would use a glue,

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:01.100
but here we're gonna try it
with a double-sided scotch.

00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:06.880
So just push it down, make sure that is
on. We do need to attach these sides,

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:11.619
but we're gonna start by trimming the
insides just to be super, super precise.

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:14.560
We're gonna lift this.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:16.660
We're.

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:20.720
Gonna line up this right on the edges
just to trim the edges just to touch.

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:26.119
So we're gonna bring this in
just so both sides are equally

00:23:26.619 --> 00:23:29.960
trimmed. Once it's lined up,
you just bring this down.

00:23:30.119 --> 00:23:34.940
It clamps down the book so it doesn't
move at all and it's ready for its trim.

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:37.080
And then we press this down.

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:42.280
So as you see it cut
off like little edges,

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:47.380
but now we have a super straight edge.
So then we're gonna take the other side.

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:52.480
It's already lined up and measured,
so all we have to do is place it,

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:56.633
bring down the clamp.

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:03.040
Most schools or binding facilities
have guillotine cutters and

00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:06.380
sometimes you can ask
to rent or borrow them.

00:24:08.300 --> 00:24:11.460
So now we have our both sides
that are super clean and flush.

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:15.680
Now we're gonna stick the front
covers in a similar fashion.

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:20.100
We're just gonna put the
double-sided tape on this.

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:27.680
For this cardboard, I'm gonna do
something a little differently.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:33.600
I'm going to remove the top and just
kind of line it up to the edge manually.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:35.500
And.

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:38.780
Then stick it down. And then
we're gonna remove the right side,

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:43.660
stick it down. I'm gonna clamp
it together and set it aside.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:50.160
And we're gonna do the same thing
on the left side. Same thing.

00:24:50.220 --> 00:24:54.140
I'm going to remove the right
side and line up the page.

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.900
And now we have our front covers,

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:04.900
so both of our front covers are here

00:25:06.540 --> 00:25:08.920
and our book is taking shape.

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:14.619
So now we have the back cover and
we need to place these on top. So

00:25:16.220 --> 00:25:19.280
I'm going to go ahead
and start with one side.

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:26.460
Okay. And we're going
to do the same thing.

00:25:27.780 --> 00:25:29.920
So I'm just going to use this first

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:34.080
and line it up.

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:36.720
All.

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:37.580
Right. And there we have it.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:42.040
The whole book is bound
and ready to open up.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:48.920
This was the idea to see this kind
of layering from left to right

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:55.220
where you can turn the pages and also
stand the book and have it open up like

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:58.100
this. So this is our design.

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:08.359
So just to show you how even when the
process of making the book is finished,

00:26:08.680 --> 00:26:11.800
I can decide to do different
iterations or try something new.

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:14.420
So this is a similar
project, similar binding,

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:19.160
and this is one that has a more complex
cover where I used barrel bolts and

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:21.359
metal industrial poles and plywood,

00:26:22.420 --> 00:26:26.680
and it also opens up and
creates a little table stand,

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:29.300
but it's the same content in the book.

00:26:30.119 --> 00:26:35.020
And so it's just interesting to see how
small design changes really change the

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:36.153
reading of the book.

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:40.400
I really enjoy creating
different iterations of the
same book using different

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:44.960
materials. There's always a continuation,
so each project leads to the next one.
